# Fix: 1-tick lag when CharacterBody stands on a moving kinematic platform (2D + 3D)

> **Note:** This is a beta-stage fix. The core idea is validated and works in standard
> scenarios, but has not been exhaustively tested across all edge cases. Feedback welcome.
>
> **Tested build:** Windows only — `scons platform=windows target=editor accesskit=no d3d12=no`
> Other platforms have not been compiled or tested.

---

## Problem

When a `CharacterBody3D` / `CharacterBody2D` (player) stands on a moving `AnimatableBody` or
another `CharacterBody` (platform), the player lags exactly **1 physics tick** behind the
platform.

Root cause — timing mismatch in Godot's physics pipeline:

```
step(N-1)
_physics_process(N) — platform     ← platform moves via move_and_collide()
_physics_process(N) — player       ← player reads platform state HERE
xform_change_list flush            ← platform's body_set_state fires HERE (too late!)
step(N)
```

When the platform calls `move_and_collide()`, `set_global_transform()` does **not** update the
physics body immediately. It queues a deferred `NOTIFICATION_TRANSFORM_CHANGED` via
`xform_change_list`, which only fires **after all `_physics_process` callbacks finish**. So the
player always reads the platform's velocity from the previous `step()`.

---

## Fix

### Part 1 — Immediate physics body push in `move_and_collide()`
`scene/3d/physics/physics_body_3d.cpp` and `scene/2d/physics/physics_body_2d.cpp`

Before calling `set_global_transform()`, directly call `body_set_state(BODY_STATE_TRANSFORM)`
on the physics server. This bypasses `xform_change_list` and immediately makes the new
transform/velocity available to `body_get_direct_state()` in the same tick.

### Part 2 — Pending transform cache in `GodotBody3D` / `GodotBody2D`
`modules/godot_physics_3d/` and `modules/godot_physics_2d/`

When `body_set_state(BODY_STATE_TRANSFORM)` is called for a kinematic body during
`_physics_process`, compute and cache the implied velocity (`pending_linear_velocity`,
`pending_angular_velocity`). The `GodotPhysicsDirectBodyState` getters return cached values
when the flag is set. The flag is cleared at the **end** of `GodotStep::step()` — not at the
beginning (step runs before `_physics_process`), and not inside `integrate_forces()` (which is
also called by `body_test_motion()` and must not clear the flag mid-frame).

Applied identically to both 3D and 2D physics stacks.

### Part 3 — Adaptive process ordering
`scene/main/scene_tree.cpp`, `scene/main/node.h`, `CharacterBody3D`, `CharacterBody2D`

`CharacterBody3D` and `CharacterBody2D` override `Node::get_physics_process_dependency()` to
return the floor platform node they are standing on. Inside `SceneTree::_process_group()`,
before processing each node, if it declares an unprocessed dependency with the **same**
`physics_process_priority` that appears later in the list, the two nodes are swapped so the
dependency runs first.

This means **no manual `process_physics_priority` configuration is needed** when platform and
player share the same priority group.

---

## Behaviour after the fix

```
step(N-1)
_physics_process(N) — platform     ← move_and_collide() → body_set_state() → pending cached ✓
_physics_process(N) — player       ← reads pending velocity = current-tick velocity ✓
xform_change_list flush            ← redundant body_set_state (same value, harmless)
step(N)                            ← end of step: pending flag cleared
```

---

## Project Setting

`Project Settings → Physics → Common → Platform Velocity Correction Beta` (default `true`)

Disabling it fully reverts to stock Godot behaviour — no early `body_set_state`, no adaptive
sorting. Zero per-frame overhead when disabled.

---

## Process ordering details

### Same priority — fully automatic

When platform and player have the **same** `physics_process_priority` (both at `0` by default),
the adaptive swap handles the ordering automatically regardless of scene tree order:

```
Scene tree:  [Player, Platform]   ← any order works

Frame result:
  Platform runs first  ✓
  Player reads current-tick velocity  ✓
```

**Stacking chains** of any depth (A on B on C …) at the same priority are handled correctly
for any initial tree order.

### Different priorities — standard behaviour, no change

When objects have different `physics_process_priority` values, Godot's standard sort already
puts them in the right order. The swap logic only fires within the same priority value, so
different-priority setups are completely unaffected.

| Node | `process_physics_priority` |
|---|---|
| Platform | `-1` (or lower than player) |
| Player | `0` (default) |

For stacked bodies requiring explicit priorities:

| Node | Priority |
|---|---|
| Bottom platform (C) | `-2` |
| Middle platform (B) | `-1` |
| Top player (A) | `0` |

### Cross-group limitation

The adaptive ordering only works within a single process group (nodes sharing the same
`process_thread_group` and priority value). Nodes in **different** groups cannot be
automatically reordered — use explicit priority in that case.

---

## `sync_to_physics` warning

**Do not use `sync_to_physics = true` on `AnimatableBody` when manually controlling it with
`move_and_collide()`.**

With `sync_to_physics = true`, the engine overwrites the node's transform each frame via
`_body_state_changed`. This conflicts with manual `move_and_collide()` and produces erratic
movement. It also bypasses the early `body_set_state()` call that populates the pending cache.

`sync_to_physics = true` is intended for bodies driven by `AnimationPlayer` or `Tween`, not
for manually scripted motion.

---

## Works for

- `AnimatableBody3D` / `AnimatableBody2D` platform + `move_and_collide()`
- `CharacterBody3D` / `CharacterBody2D` platform + `move_and_slide()`
- Rotating platforms (angular velocity cached correctly — axis-angle in 3D, scalar in 2D)
- Stacking chains of any depth (fully automatic at same priority)
- Any scene tree order when objects share the same `physics_process_priority`







